WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Recurrent neural networks are a workhorse for
sequence data,

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.045
yet they famously struggle when the data carries
long-term dependencies.

00:00:09.125 --> 00:00:13.814
This paper asks why, from the rigorous viewpoint of
approximation theory.

00:00:13.894 --> 00:00:16.951
The authors prove an inverse, or Bernstein-type,

00:00:17.031 --> 00:00:22.504
theorem for nonlinear RNNs: any sequence
relationship that can be stably approximated

00:00:22.584 --> 00:00:26.621
by an RNN must have a memory that decays
exponentially in time.

00:00:26.701 --> 00:00:31.064
In other words, the difficulty is not just an
optimization artifact,

00:00:31.144 --> 00:00:34.919
it is a fundamental limitation of the RNN
hypothesis space,

00:00:34.999 --> 00:00:39.492
extending the known curse of memory from linear to
nonlinear networks.

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:41.191
Building on this analysis,

00:00:41.271 --> 00:00:47.006
they propose a principled stable reparameterization
that lets RNNs escape the limitation,

00:00:47.086 --> 00:00:50.600
and they confirm everything with numerical experiments.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:57.424
Recurrent neural networks are among the most basic
models for learning from sequential and temporal data,

00:00:57.504 --> 00:01:01.649
with applications from time series and speech to
text and sentiment.

00:01:01.729 --> 00:01:08.049
But a long-standing empirical observation is that
they falter when the data has long-term dependencies.

00:01:08.129 --> 00:01:13.951
The open question this paper confronts is whether
that failure is only about training dynamics,

00:01:14.031 --> 00:01:18.363
like exploding or vanishing gradients, or whether
it reflects a deeper,

00:01:18.443 --> 00:01:21.842
structural limitation of what RNNs can represent at all.

00:01:21.922 --> 00:01:27.310
Answering this requires an approximation-theoretic
lens rather than an optimization one.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:31.936
Approximation theory offers two complementary kinds
of statements.

00:01:32.016 --> 00:01:33.574
Forward, or Jackson-type,

00:01:33.654 --> 00:01:38.884
theorems tell you how well a model can approximate
a sufficiently regular target.

00:01:38.964 --> 00:01:40.653
Inverse, or Bernstein-type,

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:46.290
theorems run the other direction: they assume a
target can be efficiently approximated

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:49.764
and then deduce what regularity the target must have.

00:01:49.844 --> 00:01:56.056
Inverse theorems are precisely the tool for
exposing fundamental limitations of an architecture.

00:01:56.136 --> 00:01:59.333
Earlier work proved such a result for linear RNNs,

00:01:59.413 --> 00:02:05.429
showing that efficiently approximable linear
targets must have exponentially decaying memory.

00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:08.968
The pressing question was whether adding nonlinearity,

00:02:09.048 --> 00:02:14.408
which greatly increases model capacity, would break
this so-called curse of memory.

00:02:14.488 --> 00:02:17.030
This paper was motivated to settle that.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.934
The paper makes three main contributions.

00:02:21.014 --> 00:02:24.537
First,

00:02:24.617 --> 00:02:28.141
it extends the concept of a memory function from
the linear setting to general nonlinear functional sequences,

00:02:28.221 --> 00:02:31.744
and crucially this memory function can be
numerically quantified by querying a trained model,

00:02:31.824 --> 00:02:35.348
not just defined abstractly.

00:02:35.428 --> 00:02:38.951
Second, it introduces a framework of stable approximation,

00:02:39.031 --> 00:02:42.555
a mild requirement that the approximant behaves
continuously under small parameter perturbations,

00:02:42.635 --> 00:02:46.159
which is exactly what gradient-based optimization needs.

00:02:46.239 --> 00:02:49.762
Third,

00:02:49.842 --> 00:02:53.366
using these two ingredients it proves what the
authors believe is the first Bernstein-type

00:02:53.446 --> 00:02:56.969
approximation theorem for nonlinear RNNs.

00:02:57.049 --> 00:03:00.573
On top of the theory,

00:03:00.653 --> 00:03:04.176
it proposes a principled reparameterization method
to overcome the identified limitation

00:03:04.256 --> 00:03:07.780
and confirms the whole story with experiments.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.208
The method has three pieces.

00:03:12.288 --> 00:03:16.336
First, a memory function for nonlinear functionals:
it measures,

00:03:16.416 --> 00:03:20.463
over Heaviside step inputs,

00:03:20.543 --> 00:03:24.591
how strongly the output at time t still depends on
the input, giving a precise,

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:28.719
task-independent, and numerically queryable notion
of memory.

00:03:28.799 --> 00:03:32.847
Second,

00:03:32.927 --> 00:03:36.974
a stability framework: the perturbation error at
hidden dimension m is the worst-case

00:03:37.054 --> 00:03:41.102
output error when parameters are perturbed within a
ball of radius beta,

00:03:41.182 --> 00:03:45.230
and a target is beta-zero stably approximated if
that error stays continuous up to some positive radius.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:49.358
The central theorem then says: if a target with
decaying memory is stably approximated

00:03:49.438 --> 00:03:53.486
by RNNs whose weight norms and perturbed memories
stay uniformly controlled,

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:57.613
then the target's memory must decay exponentially.

00:03:57.693 --> 00:04:01.741
The proof leverages stability to drive hidden-state
derivatives to zero and uses the Hartman-Grobman

00:04:01.821 --> 00:04:05.869
theorem to bound the recurrent eigenvalues.

00:04:05.949 --> 00:04:09.997
Finally, to overcome this curse,

00:04:10.077 --> 00:04:14.124
they substitute the recurrent weight matrix with a
continuous function that always maps into stable,

00:04:14.204 --> 00:04:18.252
negative-real-part matrices, such as an exponential
or softplus map,

00:04:18.332 --> 00:04:22.380
so the network can push eigenvalues toward zero
while remaining stable under perturbation.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.416
The experiments span synthetic and real data.

00:04:26.496 --> 00:04:30.152
On the synthetic side,

00:04:30.232 --> 00:04:33.887
the authors build linear and nonlinear functional
targets with either exponential or polynomial

00:04:33.968 --> 00:04:37.623
decaying memory and sweep the hidden dimension from
about two up to sixty four to watch

00:04:37.703 --> 00:04:41.359
how the perturbation-stability radius behaves.

00:04:41.439 --> 00:04:45.095
They also construct randomly initialized RNN
teacher models with a large hidden dimension

00:04:45.175 --> 00:04:48.831
of two hundred fifty six and approximate them with
student RNNs to test the stability

00:04:48.911 --> 00:04:52.567
filter.

00:04:52.647 --> 00:04:56.303
On real data,

00:04:56.383 --> 00:05:00.038
they query the memory function of LSTM models on
IMDB movie-review sentiment analysis,

00:05:00.118 --> 00:05:03.774
and they train nonlinear RNNs on MNIST image
classification to test the optimization benefits

00:05:03.854 --> 00:05:07.510
of stable reparameterization.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:13.572
The headline theoretical result is a clean inverse
statement: if a nonlinear RNN can stably

00:05:13.652 --> 00:05:16.295
approximate a target sequence relationship,

00:05:16.375 --> 00:05:19.524
then that target's memory must decay exponentially.

00:05:19.604 --> 00:05:24.969
This extends the linear curse of memory to the
nonlinear regime and proves the failure

00:05:25.049 --> 00:05:29.212
on long-term dependencies is intrinsic to the RNN
hypothesis space,

00:05:29.292 --> 00:05:31.048
not just a training artifact.

00:05:31.128 --> 00:05:32.694
The numerics back this up.

00:05:32.774 --> 00:05:34.594
For polynomial-memory targets,

00:05:34.674 --> 00:05:40.103
the perturbation-error curves for different hidden
dimensions keep intersecting further

00:05:40.183 --> 00:05:42.635
and further left as the dimension grows,

00:05:42.715 --> 00:05:45.548
meaning no positive stability radius survives.

00:05:45.628 --> 00:05:50.930
And when the authors filter randomly generated
teacher models to keep only those that

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.020
are both approximable and stable,

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:57.199
the only survivors are teachers whose memory decays
exponentially.

00:05:57.279 --> 00:06:02.581
They also observe that slowly-decaying targets take
on the order of a thousand epochs

00:06:02.661 --> 00:06:05.430
to fit versus about ten for exponential ones.

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:10.419
The key ablation varies only how the recurrent
weight is parameterized,

00:06:10.499 --> 00:06:12.334
holding initialization fixed.

00:06:12.414 --> 00:06:17.815
On MNIST, the three stable reparameterizations,
softplus, exponential, and inverse,

00:06:17.895 --> 00:06:22.636
all speed up optimization and reach higher test
accuracy than the direct,

00:06:22.716 --> 00:06:26.400
unstable parameterization where the weight is used as-is.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.948
Importantly, since reparameterization does not
change the model's inherent capacity,

00:06:32.028 --> 00:06:35.844
the final performance across stable variants is
comparable,

00:06:35.924 --> 00:06:41.919
isolating the effect as an optimization and
stability benefit rather than a capacity change.

00:06:41.999 --> 00:06:43.372
On the synthetic side,

00:06:43.452 --> 00:06:49.910
applying exponential and softplus
reparameterizations to linear RNNs approximating polynomial-decay

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:54.005
targets restores the expected continuous limiting
error curve,

00:06:54.085 --> 00:06:56.250
confirming stability is recovered.

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:58.693
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:06:58.773 --> 00:07:02.719
On MNIST, trained for ten epochs and averaged over
three runs,

00:07:02.799 --> 00:07:09.018
the softplus stable reparameterization reaches
seventy one point three six percent test accuracy,

00:07:09.098 --> 00:07:14.537
compared with sixty eight point four seven percent
for the direct, unstable baseline,

00:07:14.617 --> 00:07:17.719
an improvement of nearly three percentage points.

00:07:17.799 --> 00:07:22.394
The exponential and inverse stable maps also land
above seventy percent,

00:07:22.474 --> 00:07:24.537
all beating the unstable version.

00:07:24.617 --> 00:07:29.732
On the synthetic side, exponential-memory targets
are fit in roughly ten epochs,

00:07:29.812 --> 00:07:36.550
while polynomial-memory targets need on the order
of a thousand epochs and still fail the stability test.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:41.550
The teacher-filtering experiment uses hidden
dimension two hundred fifty six.

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:45.666
The one-line takeaway is this: no matter how you
train them,

00:07:45.746 --> 00:07:52.344
plain nonlinear RNNs can only stably approximate
sequence relationships whose memory fades exponentially,

00:07:52.424 --> 00:07:57.941
so their well-known struggle with long-term
dependencies is baked into the architecture,

00:07:58.021 --> 00:07:59.532
not merely the optimizer.

00:07:59.612 --> 00:08:02.139
But the same analysis points to the cure.

00:08:02.219 --> 00:08:07.800
By reparameterizing the recurrent weights with a
stable map like exponential or softplus,

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:12.761
the network can keep eigenvalues near the edge of
stability without losing it,

00:08:12.841 --> 00:08:17.086
provably relaxing the curse of memory and, on real
tasks like MNIST,

00:08:17.166 --> 00:08:19.630
training faster and generalizing better.
